#!/usr/bin/env node

// Derives the _RESTR_ANCHORS layer: one point per restriction feature, at which
// the style draws the feature's centred restriction symbol (the crossed-anchor
// ACHRES family and its siblings) instead of drawing it once per polygon per
// tile.
//
// Usage:
//   bin/generate-restriction-anchors <output.geojson> \
//     --class RESARE:RESARE.geojson [--class CBLARE:CBLARE.geojson ...] \
//     [--land LNDARE.geojson] [--area-evidence area_evidence_all.geojson] \
//     [--dsnm US5OR2JC] [--cell-floor <N>]
//
// Why this layer exists:
//
//   S-52's RESARE04/RESCSP02 render a restriction area's symbol CENTRED in the
//   area. The generated style draws that per POLYGON, and tippecanoe writes a
//   copy of each polygon into every tile it touches, so one regulated bay
//   arrives as a field of identical crossed anchors with no zoom or size gate.
//   Worse, restriction polygons genuinely span land -- a regulated area is
//   drawn to its legal boundary, not to the shoreline -- so the polygon's
//   centre is ashore for a measured 28% of them at z7, and the "do not anchor
//   here" mark sits on a hillside.
//
//   One POINT per feature lands in exactly one tile at any zoom, which is what
//   makes the symbol appear once; placing that point on the WATER-SIDE part of
//   the feature is what puts the mark on the water it regulates.
//
// Classes: RESARE, CBLARE, PIPARE, MIPARE, DMPGRD. NOT ACHARE -- anchorages
// are fine as drawn (user ruling): an anchorage is placed to its own water and
// its symbols never showed the defect.
//
// GROUPING is by (CLASS, LNAM, zoom interval). LNAM is S-57's feature
// identifier and the fragments of one feature share it within a cell, so the
// group is the FEATURE, not a contiguity guess -- two abutting but distinct
// regulated areas keep their own symbols. A feature with no LNAM is its own
// group. The interval is on the key for the reason it is on every other
// anchor generator: the copy ladder's copies of one feature OVERLAP, and
// merged they would emit one anchor stamped for no interval at all.
//
// LNAM IS NOT ENOUGH, in either direction. It is issued PER CELL, so a dumping
// ground digitised across US5OR2JC/US5OR2KC/US4OR1IF is three features with
// three LNAMs and drew three stacks of crossed anchors on one physical area (a
// PIPARE at a four-cell corner: eight) -- and one cell holds several LNAMs of
// one area just as readily, three contiguous same-content pieces of that same
// PIPARE on US5OR2KD. --area-evidence, --dsnm and --cell-floor turn on the
// ELECTION that removes both: the contiguous same-class, same-content
// component, WITHIN a cell as much as across a seam, elects the smallest DSNM
// in it and, inside that cell, the smallest group id standing on it. Every
// group is registered first and asked for its verdict second, since the
// within-cell half cannot be decided until every group has been seen.
//
// The one surviving anchor stands for the whole physical area, so it is both
// MEASURED as the whole component (AREA) and PLACED over it -- the component's
// polygons join this cell's own parts as candidates for the water-side
// placement below, because the elected cell is frequently the one holding a
// corner offcut a few square metres across. See bin/quilt-election.mjs;
// without those inputs -- an unpartitioned cell, an older cache -- every group
// keeps its own anchor exactly as before.
//
// WATER-SIDE PLACEMENT -- the approach, since it is deliberately NOT an exact
// polygon difference:
//
//   The requirement is that the anchor must not sit on land when the feature
//   has any water. An exact boolean difference against LNDARE buys nothing
//   beyond that guarantee and costs real geometry code, so the difference is
//   done on SCANLINES instead:
//
//     1. Every polygon part of the group is measured on WATER_SCAN_ROWS
//        horizontal lines spread over its own latitude extent. On each line
//        the part's interior spans (the same even-odd crossing walk
//        bin/quilt-anchors.mjs uses) have the land's interior spans at that
//        latitude SUBTRACTED as 1-D intervals -- a per-row difference against
//        LNDARE.
//     2. The surviving water spans, summed row by row, estimate each part's
//        WATER AREA, and the part with the most water is the "largest
//        water-side part" the anchor goes in.
//     3. Within that part the anchor is `pointOnSurface(part)` when that point
//        lies STRICTLY inside a water span at its own latitude (the natural
//        label point, kept when it is already wet -- strictly, so a point on
//        the shoreline itself does not count as wet), otherwise the midpoint
//        of the widest water span found -- widest first, nearest the part's
//        vertical middle on a tie -- which is an interior point of the part
//        that is not inside any LNDARE ring.
//
//   The fallback, when every part measures no water at all (a feature genuinely
//   landlocked at this cell's resolution) or when --land is absent, is the
//   plain largest-part interior point -- exactly what bin/generate-labels and
//   bin/generate-mqual-labels emit.
//
//   ACROSS CELLS the parts ranked are this cell's own CLIPPED fragments PLUS
//   the elected component's polygons -- every cell's unclipped copy of the
//   area, this one's included -- so the winner is the largest water-side part
//   of the whole physical area. --land covers only THIS cell, so a part lying
//   beyond this cell's LNDARE measures as all water and can win the ranking on
//   land it cannot see. Accepted deliberately:
//   it is precisely the knowledge the neighbour placed its own anchor with
//   before the election existed, and the alternative -- the symbol pinned
//   inside a metre-wide offcut because that offcut is all this cell holds -- is
//   the defect this replaced.
//
// Each output feature carries:
//
//   CLASS    the S-57 class the feature came from
//   RESTRN   the feature's restriction list, verbatim, absent if it has none
//   CATREA   the feature's category list, likewise
//   INTU     the chart band, likewise
//   CSCALE   the cell's compilation scale, when the export already carries it
//            (bin/stamp-quilt-zooms stamps it regardless, at step 3j)
//   AREA     the summed area in square degrees, longitude scaled by
//            cos(latitude) (bin/quilt-anchors.mjs featureArea) -- the style's
//            screen-size density floor filters on it. The whole ELECTED
//            COMPONENT's area where an election ran (its cells' unclipped
//            polygons, this one's included), so the floor measures the feature
//            rather than this cell's share of it; the group's own summed area
//            where the group stands on no component
//
// plus the copy ladder's _QZMIN/_QZMAX/_QFALL, converted to per-feature
// tippecanoe zoom bounds later by bin/stamp-quilt-zooms.

import { writeFileSync } from "fs";

import {
  ANCHOR_TIPPECANOE,
  featureArea,
  pointOnSurface,
  ringArea,
  ringCentroid,
} from "./quilt-anchors.mjs";
import { contentOf } from "./quilt-content.mjs";
import { anchorElection } from "./quilt-election.mjs";
import {
  polygons,
  quiltKey,
  quiltRange,
  readFeatures,
} from "./quilt-geojson.mjs";

const args = process.argv.slice(2);
const inputs = [];
const landPaths = [];
const evidencePaths = [];
const positional = [];
let dsnm = "";
let cellFloor = null;

for (let i = 0; i < args.length; i++) {
  if (args[i] === "--class" && args[i + 1]) {
    const value = args[++i];
    const separator = value.indexOf(":");
    inputs.push({
      className: value.slice(0, separator),
      path: value.slice(separator + 1),
    });
  } else if (args[i] === "--land" && args[i + 1]) {
    landPaths.push(args[++i]);
  } else if (args[i] === "--area-evidence" && args[i + 1]) {
    evidencePaths.push(args[++i]);
  } else if (args[i] === "--dsnm" && args[i + 1]) dsnm = args[++i];
  else if (args[i] === "--cell-floor" && args[i + 1])
    cellFloor = Number(args[++i]);
  else positional.push(args[i]);
}

const outputPath = positional[0];
if (!outputPath || inputs.length === 0) {
  console.error(
    "Usage: generate-restriction-anchors <output.geojson> " +
      "--class <CLASS>:<file.geojson> [--class ...] [--land <LNDARE.geojson>] " +
      "[--area-evidence <file.geojson>] [--dsnm <NAME>] [--cell-floor <N>]",
  );
  process.exit(1);
}

// The cross-cell election, over the other cells' semantic roster; inert without
// one. See bin/quilt-election.mjs.
const election = anchorElection({ paths: evidencePaths, dsnm, cellFloor });

/**
 * Horizontal lines each part is measured on.
 *
 * Nine rows resolve a shoreline that cuts a part into bands a ninth of its
 * height -- far finer than any charted regulated area that still has usable
 * water -- while keeping the whole pass linear in vertices. The rows sit at
 * (i + 0.5) / N of the extent so none of them lies along the part's own
 * horizontal edges, where the even-odd walk degenerates.
 */
const WATER_SCAN_ROWS = 9;

/** The land polygons, each as an array of rings, with a bbox to gate on. */
const land = landPaths
  .flatMap((path) => readFeatures(path))
  .flatMap((feature) => polygons(feature.geometry))
  .filter((polygon) => polygon[0] && polygon[0].length >= 4)
  .map((polygon) => ({ polygon, bbox: bboxOf(polygon) }));

function bboxOf(polygon) {
  let minX = Infinity;
  let minY = Infinity;
  let maxX = -Infinity;
  let maxY = -Infinity;
  for (const [x, y] of polygon[0]) {
    if (x < minX) minX = x;
    if (x > maxX) maxX = x;
    if (y < minY) minY = y;
    if (y > maxY) maxY = y;
  }
  return { minX, minY, maxX, maxY };
}

function bboxesOverlap(a, b) {
  return (
    a.minX <= b.maxX && b.minX <= a.maxX && a.minY <= b.maxY && b.minY <= a.maxY
  );
}

/**
 * The interior spans of `polygon` on the horizontal line at `y`, as sorted
 * [x0, x1] pairs -- the even-odd scanline every anchor generator uses, here as
 * the 1-D half of the land difference.
 */
function spansAt(polygon, y) {
  const crossings = [];
  for (const ring of polygon) {
    for (let i = 1; i < ring.length; i++) {
      const [x0, y0] = ring[i - 1];
      const [x1, y1] = ring[i];
      if (y0 > y !== y1 > y) {
        crossings.push(x0 + ((y - y0) * (x1 - x0)) / (y1 - y0));
      }
    }
  }
  crossings.sort((a, b) => a - b);
  const spans = [];
  for (let i = 0; i + 1 < crossings.length; i += 2) {
    spans.push([crossings[i], crossings[i + 1]]);
  }
  return spans;
}

/** `spans` minus `cuts`, both sorted interval lists on one scanline. */
function subtractSpans(spans, cuts) {
  if (cuts.length === 0) return spans;
  const out = [];
  for (const [s0, s1] of spans) {
    let start = s0;
    for (const [c0, c1] of cuts) {
      if (c1 <= start) continue;
      if (c0 >= s1) break;
      if (c0 > start) out.push([start, c0]);
      start = Math.max(start, c1);
      if (start >= s1) break;
    }
    if (start < s1) out.push([start, s1]);
  }
  return out;
}

/**
 * One part's standing against the land: its estimated water area (square
 * degrees, cos(latitude)-scaled like everything else here) and the best water
 * span found -- the widest, nearest the part's vertical middle on a tie.
 */
function waterOf(polygon) {
  const bbox = bboxOf(polygon);
  const nearby = land.filter((entry) => bboxesOverlap(bbox, entry.bbox));
  const latitude = (ringCentroid(polygon[0])[1] * Math.PI) / 180;
  const scale = Math.max(Math.cos(latitude), 0.01);

  // No land anywhere near this part: it is all water and its own area is the
  // measure. The plain interior point is already the right anchor for it.
  if (nearby.length === 0) {
    return {
      area: Math.abs(ringArea(polygon[0])) * scale,
      span: null,
      nearby,
    };
  }

  const rowHeight = (bbox.maxY - bbox.minY) / WATER_SCAN_ROWS;
  const yMid = (bbox.minY + bbox.maxY) / 2;
  let area = 0;
  let best = null;
  for (let i = 0; i < WATER_SCAN_ROWS; i++) {
    const y = bbox.minY + rowHeight * (i + 0.5);
    const water = waterSpansAt(polygon, nearby, y);
    for (const [x0, x1] of water) {
      area += (x1 - x0) * rowHeight * scale;
      const width = x1 - x0;
      if (
        !best ||
        width > best.width ||
        (width === best.width && Math.abs(y - yMid) < Math.abs(best.y - yMid))
      ) {
        best = { width, y, x: (x0 + x1) / 2 };
      }
    }
  }
  return { area, span: best, nearby };
}

/** The part's spans at `y` with the nearby land's subtracted. */
function waterSpansAt(polygon, nearby, y) {
  const cuts = nearby
    .flatMap((entry) => spansAt(entry.polygon, y))
    .sort((a, b) => a[0] - b[0]);
  return subtractSpans(spansAt(polygon, y), cuts);
}

/**
 * Whether a point lies STRICTLY inside a water span at its own latitude.
 *
 * Strictly, so a point exactly on the shoreline -- which the even-odd walk
 * reads as outside land -- still does not count as wet: the whole reason the
 * anchor moved is to be ON the water, not on the boundary of it.
 */
function strictlyOnWater(polygon, nearby, point) {
  if (nearby.length === 0) return true;
  return waterSpansAt(polygon, nearby, point[1]).some(
    ([x0, x1]) => point[0] > x0 && point[0] < x1,
  );
}

/**
 * The anchor of one group of parts: an interior point of the largest
 * water-side part, or of the plainly largest part when there is no water to
 * find (see the header for the whole rule).
 *
 * `parts` are this cell's own CLIPPED fragments FOLLOWED BY the elected
 * component's polygons, ranked together -- the order matters only for a tie,
 * which this cell's own geometry then keeps.
 */
function anchorOf(parts) {
  if (land.length > 0) {
    let bestPart = null;
    let bestWater = 0;
    for (const part of parts) {
      const water = waterOf(part.polygon);
      if (water.area > bestWater) {
        bestWater = water.area;
        bestPart = { ...part, water };
      }
    }
    if (bestPart) {
      // The natural label point, kept whenever it is already wet -- the
      // scanline midpoint is the fallback for a part whose interior point is
      // ashore (or on the shoreline itself), not a replacement for one that
      // is not.
      const surface = pointOnSurface(bestPart.polygon);
      if (strictlyOnWater(bestPart.polygon, bestPart.water.nearby, surface)) {
        return surface;
      }
      const span = bestPart.water.span;
      if (span) return [span.x, span.y];
    }
  }

  // No land file, or every part measured dry: the plain largest-part interior
  // point, exactly as bin/generate-labels places a name.
  let biggest = null;
  let biggestArea = -1;
  for (const part of parts) {
    const area = Math.abs(ringArea(part.polygon[0]));
    if (area > biggestArea) {
      biggestArea = area;
      biggest = part;
    }
  }
  return biggest ? pointOnSurface(biggest.polygon) : undefined;
}

/** The attributes carried verbatim onto the anchor, when the source has them. */
const CARRIED_PROPERTIES = ["RESTRN", "CATREA", "INTU", "CSCALE"];

// ---- 1. group every feature by (CLASS, LNAM, interval) ---------------------

const groups = new Map();
let anonymous = 0;

for (const { className, path } of inputs) {
  for (const feature of readFeatures(path)) {
    const parts = polygons(feature.geometry)
      .filter((polygon) => polygon[0] && polygon[0].length >= 4)
      .map((polygon) => ({ polygon }));
    if (parts.length === 0) continue;

    const properties = feature.properties ?? {};
    const range = quiltRange(properties);
    // A feature with no LNAM has no identity to group on and is its own group
    // -- `#n` cannot collide with a real LNAM, which is hex.
    const lnam = properties.LNAM ?? `#${anonymous++}`;
    const id = `${className}␀${lnam}␀${quiltKey(range)}`;

    let group = groups.get(id);
    if (!group) {
      group = {
        className,
        range,
        parts: [],
        area: 0,
        carried: {},
        // The identity the election compares across cells, from the FIRST
        // fragment: the fragments of one LNAM are one feature and carry one
        // content.
        content: contentOf(properties),
      };
      groups.set(id, group);
    }
    group.parts.push(...parts);
    group.area += featureArea(feature.geometry);
    for (const name of CARRIED_PROPERTIES) {
      const value = properties[name];
      if (value !== null && value !== undefined && !(name in group.carried)) {
        group.carried[name] = value;
      }
    }
  }
}

// ---- 2. register every group with the election ------------------------------

// PASS 1, before any verdict is asked for. The election elects among ALL the
// groups standing on one component -- the several LNAMs this cell holds of one
// physical area as much as the other cells' parts of it -- so it cannot answer
// for any of them until it has been shown every one. See bin/quilt-election.mjs.
for (const [id, group] of groups) {
  election.register(
    id,
    group.className,
    group.content,
    group.parts.map((part) => part.polygon),
  );
}

// ---- 3. one anchor per elected component ------------------------------------

const features = [];
let elsewhere = 0;

for (const [id, group] of groups) {
  // The election: one anchor for the whole physical area, emitted by the cell
  // the component elected and, inside that cell, by the first of the groups
  // standing on it. A group alone in its component elects itself and nothing
  // changes.
  const verdict = election.verdict(id);
  if (!verdict.emit) {
    elsewhere++;
    continue;
  }
  // Placed over the WHOLE component, not over this cell's share of it: the
  // elected cell's share can be a corner offcut a few metres across, and an
  // anchor inside it sits at the extreme edge of the physical area (at overzoom
  // visibly outside it). The component's parts are the roster's unclipped
  // polygons; own clipped parts first, so a tie stays in this cell.
  const anchor = anchorOf([
    ...group.parts,
    ...verdict.parts.map((polygon) => ({ polygon })),
  ]);
  if (!anchor) continue;
  features.push({
    type: "Feature",
    // See ANCHOR_TIPPECANOE: one anchor per feature, exempt from dot-dropping.
    tippecanoe: ANCHOR_TIPPECANOE,
    geometry: { type: "Point", coordinates: anchor },
    properties: {
      CLASS: group.className,
      ...group.carried,
      // The whole component's area, not this cell's share of it: the style's
      // screen-size floor has to measure the FEATURE, and the elected cell's
      // share can be a corner offcut a few metres across. The verdict's area
      // is the component's polygons INCLUDING this cell's own unclipped row,
      // so it replaces the local sum rather than adding to it; the local sum
      // is what a group standing on no component keeps.
      AREA: verdict.parts.length > 0 ? verdict.area : group.area,
      // The interval the group's members serve, converted to tippecanoe
      // bounds later by bin/stamp-quilt-zooms.
      ...group.range,
    },
  });
}

writeFileSync(
  outputPath,
  JSON.stringify({ type: "FeatureCollection", features }),
);
console.error(
  `Generated ${features.length} restriction anchors` +
    (elsewhere > 0 ? `, ${elsewhere} elected elsewhere` : ""),
);
